---
/**
 * The platform's wordmark: a drawn `D` followed by "ezabin" in the site's own
 * font. One component, so the logo can never drift between the header, the auth
 * pages and whatever comes next.
 *
 * WHY THE D IS A DRAWING AND NOT A LETTER (CURRENT_TASK סשן ב׳, 2026-08-05).
 * It is the right half of an octagon: a straight back, and a bowl made of three
 * cut edges. Everything about it is matched to the wordmark's own font by
 * MEASUREMENT rather than by eye — the numbers below were read off the font by
 * rendering it to a canvas and scanning pixels, because two earlier attempts at
 * "looks about right" were both visibly wrong (too light, then too narrow).
 *
 * WEIGHT 600, TRACKING ZERO (owner's call, סשן ג׳, 2026-08-05). It was Heebo 700
 * at -0.03em and it read as bold interface text rather than as a mark: 700 is
 * the weight of every heading and button on this site, so the wordmark did not
 * separate from the furniture around it. The tracking went with it — closing a
 * word up is a headline's language, and at display size it is what read as
 * cheap. Uppercase and several other families were drawn and compared first;
 * this is the setting that was chosen. No new font ships for it: 600 is already
 * one of the ten preloaded faces.
 *
 * EVERY NUMBER BELOW MOVED WITH THE WEIGHT, because they are all Heebo's own,
 * re-measured at 600. The same scan reproduces the previous figures exactly at
 * 700, which is what says it is the method that produced them:
 *
 *   cap height        28 units in this viewBox   (0.71em — a weight can't move it)
 *   back (vertical)   5.127 = 0.1831 of cap — Heebo 600's stem  (700: 0.2113)
 *   bowl's right side 5.127 — a straight edge beside a straight edge; a lighter
 *                             one reads as a mistake rather than as contrast
 *   diagonals         4.633 = 0.9037 of the stem — a diagonal looks lighter than
 *                             its measured width
 *   horizontals       4.096 = 0.799 of the stem — a horizontal looks heavier
 *   letter width      21.098 = 0.7535 of cap, exactly Heebo 600's D  (700: 0.7676)
 *   counter           0.514 of the letter's width, against the font's own 0.5093
 *
 * The two optical ratios (0.799 and 0.9037) are deliberately CARRIED OVER from
 * the 700 drawing rather than re-measured: they correct for how an eye reads a
 * horizontal and a diagonal, which is not a fact about a weight. The stem is the
 * only measured input; everything else is derived from it.
 *
 * THE `0.0356em` BETWEEN THE D AND THE `e` is the same idea. Heebo 600's own
 * "De" leaves 0.0869em of ink gap (the D's right side bearing 0.0483 plus the
 * e's left bearing 0.0386), and this SVG carries 0.5 units of padding inside its
 * viewBox — 0.0127em at this height — so the margin is what is left over. It
 * used to be NEGATIVE, which was right then for two reasons that both changed:
 * 700's bearings are tighter, and -0.03em of tracking was shrinking every other
 * gap in the word, so the mark had to shrink with them. A mark spaced like a
 * symbol instead of like a letter is what made earlier versions read as "an icon
 * glued next to a word" — and that is true in either direction.
 *
 * COLOUR. On a light surface the letters carry the site's brand gradient — the
 * one `.btn` uses, unchanged — as ONE ramp across the whole wordmark rather than
 * per letter. The D covers the first 16.7% of the wordmark's width, so it gets
 * exactly that slice (#2a3c40 → #2d4045) and the text continues from there.
 * Giving the D its own full gradient makes it end light and the `e` restart
 * dark, which reads as two pieces stuck together.
 *
 * Height is `0.71em`, not `1cap`: Heebo's cap ratio is a known, measured
 * constant here, and hard-coding it means the mark can't shift if the font
 * fails to load and a fallback with different metrics paints for a frame.
 *
 * ACCESSIBILITY. The `D` is a drawing and the rest is text, so the DOM says
 * "ezabin" — a screen reader, and anything else reading the accessible name,
 * would announce the brand without its first letter. The whole lockup is
 * therefore one `role="img"` with the real name on it, and everything inside is
 * hidden from the tree. The visible text stays real text (it is what gives the
 * mark the font's own kerning and what keeps it sharp at any zoom); it is simply
 * not what the name is read from.
 */
interface Props {
  /** `brand` = the site gradient inside the letters (light surfaces).
   *  `white` = solid white, for a brand-coloured surface. */
  tone?: 'brand' | 'white';
  /** Any CSS length. Everything else is in `em`, so this is the only size knob;
   *  omit it to inherit (the header sizes the logo through `.logo`). */
  size?: string;
  /** Adds the Hebrew line under the name. Off by default: in a header or a form
   *  it would be a second voice in a place that has room for one. */
  tagline?: boolean;
  class?: string;
}

const { tone = 'brand', size, tagline = false, class: className = '' } = Astro.props;

/** The accessible name. Not `store.name` from the config: this component draws
 *  these exact seven letters, so the two must not be able to disagree. */
const NAME = 'Dezabin';
const TAGLINE = 'מתחם חנויות דיגיטלי';

const toneClass =
  tone === 'white'
    ? 'text-white'
    : 'bg-[linear-gradient(135deg,#2a3c40,#3a5260)] bg-clip-text text-transparent';
---

<span
  role="img"
  aria-label={tagline ? `${NAME} — ${TAGLINE}` : NAME}
  class:list={[
    // `dz-logo` carries no styling — it is a stable hook, because the rest of
    // this class list is utilities and a stylesheet cannot address those. The
    // header needs to nudge THIS element and nothing else near it.
    'dz-logo',
    'inline-flex flex-col items-start gap-[0.05em] align-middle',
    toneClass,
    className,
  ]}
  style={size ? `font-size:${size}` : undefined}
>
  <span dir="ltr" class="inline-flex items-baseline font-semibold leading-none whitespace-nowrap text-[1em]">
  {/* `max-w-none` is not decoration: reset.css sets `svg { max-width:100% }`,
      which clamps a fixed-size inline SVG inside a narrow box — the same trap
      that shrank icons in the products table. `shrink-0` does not cover it,
      because max-width is not flex shrinking. */}
  <svg
    class="h-[0.71em] w-auto max-w-none shrink-0 block me-[0.0356em]"
    viewBox="10.75 8 22.1 28"
    aria-hidden="true"
    focusable="false"
  >
    {tone === 'brand' && (
      <defs>
        {/* The D's slice of the shared ramp — see the header comment. */}
        <linearGradient id="dz-d-grad" x1="0" y1="0" x2="1" y2="1">
          <stop offset="0" stop-color="#2a3c40" />
          <stop offset="1" stop-color="#2d4045" />
        </linearGradient>
      </defs>
    )}
    <path
      fill={tone === 'white' ? '#fff' : 'url(#dz-d-grad)'}
      fill-rule="evenodd"
      d="M11.25 8 L24.148 8 L32.348 16.2 L32.348 27.8 L24.148 36 L11.25 36 Z
         M16.377 12.096 L21.692 12.096 L27.221 17.625 L27.221 26.375 L21.692 31.904 L16.377 31.904 Z"
    />
    </svg><span class="text-[1em] leading-none" aria-hidden="true">ezabin</span>
  </span>
  {tagline && (
    /* HEEBO, AT ITS NATURAL SPACING — both halves of that are the fix (2026-08-05).
     *
     * It was Rubik 600 stretched with +0.0356em of tracking to reach the name's
     * width. Two problems, one cause. Hebrew is drawn to sit close: tracking it
     * open does not enlarge it, it loosens it, and a loosened Hebrew word under a
     * tight Latin wordmark is what reads as amateur (owner: "לא מספיק מקצועי ולא
     * מספיק מתאים לאתר"). And a second family for one line on one page fails the
     * first design-line test — is it already in the system — while costing an
     * eleventh font preload on EVERY page and a second face that `font-display:
     * optional` can refuse at first paint. Rubik and Heebo are siblings anyway
     * (Heebo is Rubik's Latin with Hebrew drawn for it), so the family was buying
     * almost no difference for all of that.
     *
     * The width match is kept — it is what makes this a lockup rather than two
     * stacked lines — but it is now solved with SIZE at zero tracking instead of
     * with tracking at a fixed size.
     *
     * IT IS 0.409em SINCE THE NAME WENT TO 600 AT ZERO TRACKING, replacing the
     * 0.39em solved earlier the same day. The number is not a preference, it is the
     * wordmark's width divided by the tagline's: dropping -0.03em of tracking and
     * a weight both widen the name, so its ink went from 3.308em to 3.4886em
     * (the D's 0.535 + the 0.0869 gap + "ezabin" at 2.8667), while Heebo 500's
     * own "מתחם חנויות דיגיטלי" is 8.481em as before. 3.4886/8.481 = 0.4113.
     *
     * AND IT IS 0.409 AND NOT THAT 0.4113, which is the whole point — the same
     * point the previous value was picked on. Equality to a fraction of a pixel
     * is exactly what device-pixel rounding then resolves in whichever direction
     * it likes; the owner caught the old one by dropping a straight line from the
     * D onto a screenshot and finding the final yod of דיגיטלי a full pixel
     * outside it. The target is not "equal" but "never outside". Re-swept the
     * same 22 viewports from 320 to 1600 at DPR 1, 2 and 3: 0.4113 crosses (worst
     * -0.05px at 320/3x), 0.411 is the largest that never does and only just
     * (0.33px), and 0.409 leaves the yod 1.02px INSIDE the D everywhere with the
     * מ flush on the right. That is the same pixel of headroom the previous value
     * was chosen for. Re-run the sweep if the name's weight or tracking, or the
     * D's own width, ever move again — all three feed this one number.
     *
     * Trailing zeros are not decorative here: Tailwind emits no rule at all for
     * `text-[0.390em]`, so the class silently does nothing and the line renders at
     * the inherited size. Write a value with no trailing zero.
     *
     * Weight 500, not 600: the name above is 600 now, so this is the one place
     * where the two would be the same weight — and at 41% of the size, matching
     * weights make the line read as a second name rather than as a subtitle.
     *
     * No negative margin any more. That existed only to cancel the letter-space
     * CSS adds after the last character; at zero tracking there is none. */
    <span
      dir="rtl"
      class="font-medium text-[0.409em] leading-none"
      aria-hidden="true"
    >{TAGLINE}</span>
  )}
</span>
